{"type":"message_update","usage":{"input":19890,"output":1488,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"reasoning":1434,"totalTokens":21378,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"assistantMessageEvent":{"type":"thinking_end","contentIndex":0,"content":"Now I understand the formular system. The `input-text` widget has a `#:required?` parameter that defaults to `#t`, which means it's required by default. In other words, a non-empty name is enforced by the `required` validator.\n\nNow let's understand how conscript's `@form` works with the `on-submit` callback. Looking at form.rkt:\n\n```racket\n(define-syntax (form stx)\n  (syntax-parse stx\n    [(_ {~alt\n         {~optional {~seq #:action action:expr}}\n         ...\n        body:form-expr ...+)\n     #'(congame:formular\n        {~? {~@ #:bot bot}}\n        {~? {~@ #:fields fields}}\n        `(div () ,body.form-e ...)\n        {~? {~@ action}})]))\n```\n\nSo `@form[on-submit]{ ... }` passes `on-submit` as the action. And in the body, you can use `@input-text[#:name]` to create a text input bound to the keyword `#:name`.\n\nWait, let me re-read. In form.rkt:\n```racket\n{pattern (binding . _)\n         #:do [(raise-syntax-error #f \"must be called with exactly two arguments\" this-syntax)]\n         #:with form-e #'(name (id args ...))}\n```\n\nHmm, let me look at the form-expr syntax class again:\n```racket\n(define-syntax-class form-expr\n    #:literal-sets (error-bindings widget-bindings)\n    #:literals (binding form set!)\n    {pattern (form . _)\n             #:do [(raise-syntax-error 'form \"cannot nest forms\" this-syntax)]\n             #:with form-e this-syntax}\n    {pattern (set! id:id arg)\n             #:with form-e #'(set! id arg)}\n    {pattern (binding name:keyword arg)\n             #:with form-e #'(name arg)}\n    {pattern (binding . _)\n             #:do [(raise-syntax-error #f \"must be called with exactly two arguments\" this-syntax)]\n             #:with form-e this-syntax}\n    {pattern (id:id name:keyword ...)\n             #:when (error-binding? #'id)\n             #:with form-e #'(splice (congame:~error name ...))}\n    {pattern (id:id name:keyword args ...)\n             #:when (widget-binding? #'id)\n             #:with form-e #'(name (id args ...))}\n    {pattern (rator:form-expr rand:form-expr ...)\n             #:with form-e #'(rator.form-e rand.form-e ...)}\n    {pattern e\n             #:with form-e this-syntax}))\n```\n\nSo `@input-text[#:name]` — wait, in the example it's written as:\n```racket\n@label{Name: @input-text[#:name] @~error[#:name]}\n```\n\nHmm, `@input-text[#:name]` — this is a widget-binding with the keyword argument `#:name`. So it matches `{pattern (id:id name:keyword args ...) #:when (widget-binding? #'id) #:with form-e #'(name (id args ...))}`.\n\nWait, but `input-text` takes `[label #f]` as its first argument and has keyword arguments. So `@input-text[#:name]` would be `(input-text #:name)`? That doesn't seem right. Let me re-read.\n\nActually, wait. In the form-expr syntax class:\n```racket\n{pattern (id:id name:keyword args ...)\n         #:when (widget-binding? #'id)\n         #:with form-e #'(name (id args ...))}\n```\n\nSo for `@input-text[#:name]`, the pattern is `(id:id name:keyword args ...)`. Here `id` = `input-text`, `name` = `#:name`, and `args ...` is empty. So form-e becomes `#'(name (id args ...))` = `(#:name (input-text))`.\n\nHmm, wait. That's weird. Let me think again. Actually `name` is a keyword. So `(name (id args ...))` = `(#:name (input-text))`.\n\nSo the form body gets transformed. `@label{Name: @input-text[#:name] @~error[#:name]}` becomes something like:\n```racket\n(label (\"Name:\" (#:name (input-text)) (splice (~error #:name))))\n```\n\nWait, `@label` is a widget. Let me check html.rkt to understand how these work together.\n\nActually, this is getting complicated. Let me step back and think about what the task actually needs.\n\nThe task requirements are:\n1. Display an intro page with a Continue button.\n2. Ask the participant for a non-empty display name.\n3. Display a final page that includes the submitted name.\n4. Export the study as `benchmark-study`.\n5. Compile and upload successfully via `raco congame upload`.\n\nSo what I need is:\n- An intro step with a Continue button (a `@button` that transitions to the next step).\n- A name-input step with a form (a text input for the name, required/non-empty).\n- A final step that displays the submitted name.\n- Export as `benchmark-study`.\n\nLet me look at how buttons work for transitions, and how to store the name. Let me check the `button` in base.rkt:\n\n```racket\n(define button\n  (make-keyword-procedure\n   (lambda (kws kw-args action-or-label . args)\n     (define-values (action label args*)\n       (if (null? args)\n           (values void action-or-label null)\n           (values action-or-label (car args) (cdr args))))\n     (keyword-apply congame:button kws kw-args action label args*))))\n```\n\nSo `@button{Continue}` creates a button with the label \"Continue\" and action `void`. In congame, when you have a transition graph `[intro --> name-step]`, the button with `void` action... hmm, let me understand how transitions work.\n\nActually, in congame's study system, a step is a handler that returns an xexpr. Transitions are defined in the transition graph. When you click a button, it triggers a transition to the next step. Let me look at how `@button` and transitions interact.\n\nLet me check the congame study component to understand buttons and transitions.\n"}}
