{"type":"turn_end","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Now I have a solid understanding of the system. Let me summarize what I've learned:\n\n1. `defstep (name) body` defines a step with an id and handler.\n2. `@button{Continue}` creates a button that, when clicked, calls the action (void) and then continues to the next step in the transition graph.\n3. `@form[on-submit]{ ... }` creates a form with an action callback.\n4. `defvar name` defines a variable that can be read/written via the database (get/put).\n5. `defstudy id [step1 --> step2 ...]` defines a study with transitions.\n\nNow, the key insight for the \"non-empty display name\" requirement:\n- The `input-text` widget has a `#:required?` parameter that defaults to `#t`. So by default, the text input is required (non-empty).\n- When using `@form[on-submit]{ @input-text[#:name] ... }`, the form will validate that `#:name` is non-empty before calling `on-submit`.\n\nWait, let me double check. In the conscript form.rkt:\n```racket\n{pattern (id:id name:keyword args ...)\n         #:when (widget-binding? #'id)\n         #:with form-e #'(name (id args ...))}\n```\n\nSo `@input-text[#:name]` becomes `(#:name (input-text))`. And in the formular, `(kwd fld)` where kwd is a keyword and fld is the field. The `input-text` with no label creates a required text input by default (`#:required? #t`).\n\nSo the form will enforce that `name` is non-empty. \n\nNow let me think about how to store the name and display it on the final page.\n\nLooking at the kitchen-sink example:\n```racket\n(defvar name)\n\n(defstep (tell-name)\n  (define (on-submit #:name the-name)\n    (set! name the-name))\n  @html{@h1{What is your name?}\n        @form[on-submit]{\n          @label{Name: @text-field[name]}\n          @submit-button[]\n        }})\n```\n\nWait, this uses `@text-field[name]` which is different from `@input-text[#:name]`. Let me check what `text-field` is. Actually, in the kitchen-sink example it uses `@form[on-submit]` with `@text-field[name]`. Hmm, but the form.rkt example uses `@input-text[#:name]`.\n\nLet me check html.rkt to see what widgets are available.\n","thinkingSignature":"reasoning_content"},{"type":"text","text":"\n\n"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"546328732","name":"read","arguments":{"path":"/workspace/../reference/conscript/html.rkt"}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"lmstudio","model":"qwen3.8-27b-mlx","usage":{"input":34729,"output":606,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"reasoning":573,"totalTokens":35335,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1787225392455,"responseId":"chatcmpl-xr6f0vw03a862t1ubxgsb","rawStopReason":"tool_calls"},"toolResults":[{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"546328732","toolName":"read","content":[{"type":"text","text":"#lang racket/base\n\n(require (for-syntax racket/base\n                     syntax/parse/pre)\n         (except-in congame/components/study form)\n         racket/match\n         \"html-element.rkt\")\n\n(provide\n (all-from-out \"html-element.rkt\")\n html\n html*)\n\n(define-syntax (html stx)\n  (syntax-parse stx\n    [(_ elt ...)\n     #'`(div ([class \"container\"]) ,(html* elt ...))]))\n\n(define-syntax (html* stx)\n  (syntax-parse stx\n    [(_ elt ...)\n     #'(paragraphify `(div () ,elt ...))]))\n\n(define (paragraphify? e)\n  (memq (car e) '(aside div form section)))\n\n(define (swallow? e)\n  (match e\n    [(? string?) #t]\n    [`(,id ,_ . ,_)\n     (memq id '(a blockquote button em img input label select span strong textarea u))]\n    [_ #f]))\n\n(define (paragraphify e)\n  (cond\n    [(paragraphify? e)\n     (match-define `(,id ,attrs . ,content) e)\n     (define (commit p-content c)\n       (cons `(p () . ,(reverse p-content)) c))\n     (define para-content\n       (for/fold ([para-content null]\n                  [paragraph null]\n                  [newlines 0]\n                  #:result (reverse\n                       